    "description": "Iran’s physical geography combines mountain belts, interior basins, desert surfaces, and coastal lowlands. Their relationships help explain regional differences in drainage, vegetation, and the availability of water. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Physical geography", "Landforms", "Mountain belts", "Drainage", "Deserts"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:46:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:46:38Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Physical Geography: Mountains, Basins and Deserts | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand how Iran’s mountain belts, interior basins, drainage systems and desert processes fit together."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "landscape-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A country organized around relief",
        "content": "Iran’s landscape is easier to understand as a collection of connected mountain and basin systems than as a uniform plateau. The Zagros dominates the western and southwestern mountain margin; northern ranges follow the southern Caspian region; other mountains interrupt the eastern interior. Between them lie basins containing salt flats, stony ground, sandy surfaces, and more cultivable land near mountain foothills. FAO’s historical country profile describes this broad arrangement, although its agricultural and population statistics belong to an earlier reporting period. [s1]\n\nRelief also helps organize the country’s water. The same profile distinguishes drainage toward the Caspian Sea, the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman, and inland receiving areas including the Central Plateau, Lake Urmia, and Hamun. A drainage basin is the area contributing water to a particular outlet or receiving depression. These divisions offer a different way to read a map from provincial boundaries: they follow where water can travel. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mountain-building",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Mountains shaped by different tectonic processes",
        "content": "The Zagros belongs to the collision zone between Arabia and Eurasia. Compression has produced a fold-and-thrust belt: rock layers bend into folds and move over one another along thrust faults. Farther southeast, offshore from Iran and Pakistan, the Makran margin involves subduction, where the Arabian plate descends beneath Eurasia. These are distinct expressions of plate convergence, rather than one identical mountain-building mechanism extending around Iran. [s2]\n\nNASA’s interpretation of astronaut photography shows how this structure becomes visible in southern Zagros scenery. Elongated folded limestone ridges alternate with intervening valleys. Smaller, roughly circular salt domes interrupt the pattern. Salt is less dense than the rocks above it and can rise through them, producing local uplifts. A useful distinction follows: the age of the rock is different from the age of the folding that shaped its present arrangement. [s3]\n\nThe Alborz has its own pattern of active deformation. Geodetic research documents both compression and sideways movement along faults, with differences between western and eastern sectors. The mountain belts therefore require regional explanations; their similar appearance as high ground does not make their structures interchangeable. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "desert-surfaces",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Desert is a family of landforms",
        "content": "The Lut Desert demonstrates why an arid landscape should not be pictured simply as sand dunes. UNESCO describes a mountain-surrounded interior basin containing wind-carved ridges, stony desert pavements, dune fields, and salt features. Yardangs, locally associated with the name kaluts, are elongated forms sculpted as wind-driven sand abrades the ground. Dunes instead record the accumulation and reshaping of transported sand. Erosion and deposition can therefore create sharply different surfaces within the same desert. [s5]\n\nWater also participates in this dry landscape. Intermittent streams transport sediment, while evaporation leaves dissolved minerals behind as salt crusts and other evaporite formations. Aridity does not mean that water has played no part in shaping the terrain. Nor does the presence of sand imply that the whole basin has the same surface, origin, or response to wind. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "landform-process-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Processes behind contrasting landforms",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Arabia-Eurasia convergence\"] -->|\"Compression and folding\"| B[\"Zagros fold-and-thrust belt\"]\n  C[\"Mobile salt beneath overlying rock\"] -->|\"Upward movement\"| D[\"Salt domes\"]\n  E[\"Wind-driven sand in the Lut\"] -->|\"Abrasion\"| F[\"Yardangs\"]\n  E -->|\"Transport and accumulation\"| G[\"Dune fields\"]\n  H[\"Mineral-bearing water\"] -->|\"Evaporation\"| I[\"Salt crusts\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows identify specific formation processes. The diagram separates tectonic deformation, salt movement, wind erosion, sediment accumulation, and mineral precipitation; it is not a chronological sequence.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How the regional pages fit together",
        "content": "The Iranian Plateau page explains the broader framework and the distinction between plateau, basin, and mountain margin. The Alborz and Zagros pages examine different mountain systems. Dasht-e Kavir, the Lut Desert, and the Central Plains provide more focused entry points into the interior. Read together, these subjects replace a single image of Iran with a set of regional landscapes connected by rock structure, sediment movement, and drainage. [s1][s2][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s5"]
      }
    ],
