    "description": "The Iranian Plateau is a broad highland framework whose mountain margins and interior basins contain contrasting environments. Its dry interior includes foothill zones where groundwater engineering has supported settlement and agriculture. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Iranian Plateau", "Interior basins", "Foothills", "Groundwater", "Qanats"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:46:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:46:38Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "The Iranian Plateau: Basins, Mountain Margins and Water | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the Iranian Plateau as a varied highland region, with interior basins, mountain foothills and gravity-fed qanat systems."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning-and-scale",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the name describes",
        "content": "The Iranian Plateau is a regional geographical framework rather than a synonym for a flat surface. Scholarly historical geography describes it through contrasts between mountain chains, arid basins, and intermediate foothills. In this usage, its significance lies in the relationships among different environments, rather than in a single uniform elevation or landscape. [s1]\n\nTerminology also changes with scale. FAO’s Iran profile uses “Central or Interior Plateau” for the interior between surrounding mountain chains. That narrower national description should be distinguished from the broader Iranian highland framework used in regional geography. This page concentrates on the Iranian interior and its mountain margins; it does not assign a precise international boundary to the wider plateau. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "basins-and-foothills",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An interior with contrasting surfaces",
        "content": "Within the interior, salt-affected depressions, sandy or stony ground, and foothill land occur in different combinations. These are not interchangeable surfaces. FAO’s description explicitly places more favorable land near the surrounding mountains, while scholarly geography emphasizes foothills as an intermediate environment between wetter uplands and desert basins. [s1][s2]\n\nThe distinction helps explain why an apparently open landscape can contain sharp local differences in opportunities for cultivation. A basin floor, a valley, and the foot of a mountain occupy different positions within the same regional system. The plateau is therefore best read through these smaller units instead of treating its entire interior as one desert. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "qanat-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How groundwater connects uplands and settlements",
        "content": "A qanat is an underground water-collection and conveyance system that uses gravity. UNESCO’s documentation describes tunnels tapping alluvial aquifers, meaning groundwater held in sediments deposited by flowing water, commonly beneath alluvial fans near valley heads. An alluvial fan is a spreading sediment deposit at a mountain front. The gently sloping tunnel carries collected water toward a surface outlet and onward into distribution channels. [s3]\n\nVertical shafts along the route allow excavation, ventilation, and maintenance. Their alignment can make an otherwise hidden water system legible at the surface. The essential engineering relationship is between the groundwater source, the tunnel gradient, and the lower outlet: the system transports existing groundwater rather than creating a new supply. [s3]\n\nUNESCO connects qanat distribution with settlement on the inner plateau’s alluvial fans and documents associated reservoirs, mills, gardens, and communal management practices. These examples show how physical geography and technical knowledge worked together. They do not establish that every plateau settlement used a qanat or that every surviving system has the same condition today. Understanding the plateau requires attention both to its landforms and to the particular ways people obtained water within them. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "qanat-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The gravity-fed connection",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Alluvial aquifer\"] -->|\"Groundwater enters\"| B[\"Collection tunnel\"]\n  B -->|\"Gentle gradient carries water\"| C[\"Surface outlet\"]\n  C -->|\"Water distributed through\"| D[\"Irrigation channels\"]\n  D -->|\"Supply\"| E[\"Cultivated land\"]\n  F[\"Vertical access shafts\"] -->|\"Enable excavation and maintenance of\"| B\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram explains the water pathway and the separate maintenance function of access shafts. It is a schematic of a qanat, not a claim that all plateau groundwater follows this route.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
